diff --git a/docs/report-validation-and-retention.md b/docs/report-validation-and-retention.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3670831
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/report-validation-and-retention.md
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+# Report validation and compact chart retention
+
+3 October 2026. The collector validates the known block, stats and pending
+fields before changing node values, receipt timestamps or chart history.
+Invalid history batches are rejected together and no longer clear a pending
+history request. The existing fifty-block reply cap also applies at collection.
+
+Block reports require hashes/roots, producer address, timestamp, gas fields,
+difficulty/total difficulty, transaction hash objects and an uncle array.
+Optional ticket counts preserve null/missing versus zero. Stats also require
+numeric gas price and hashrate. Unknown report kinds are rejected.
+
+This is a dashboard wire profile, not block or consensus verification. Numeric
+counters must be nonnegative JavaScript safe integers. Timestamps must remain
+safe after conversion to milliseconds. Difficulty accepts a safe integer or
+canonical nonnegative decimal text of up to 78 digits. Header quantities in
+nonempty uncle arrays use canonical hexadecimal, up to 64 digits for big
+integers and 16 for uint64 fields; byte fields follow efsn's header JSON shape.
+Gas used cannot exceed gas limit. These limits must be included in deployment
+compatibility review; they neither impose chain rules nor prove a report true.
+
+The chart cache retains a new record containing only the scalar fields used
+for charts, fork comparison and propagation, plus transaction/uncle counts.
+It retains neither transaction hashes, uncle header objects nor extensions.
+Mutating the original report cannot alter a cached record. The existing
+2,000-height window and per-identity fork retention/representative policy stay
+in place. Counts are derived once when discarding the arrays, not stored as
+additional copies beside those arrays.
+
+Current node snapshots and the `/blocks` API still contain the validated head's
+transaction hashes, uncle headers and receipt/propagation metadata. Unrecognized
+extensions remain in the current-head report for compatibility, bounded by the
+configured message budget; they are not admitted to the chart cache. Node
+registration metadata and ping payloads retain their existing handling and
+need separate field/byte-budget review before public deployment.
+
+Evidence: eight new regressions fail against the previous production sources.
+The candidate passes 159 contract tests, 12 loopback socket tests, six database
+scenarios and actual synthetic efsn/PG/API/compiled-browser integration. The
+actual fixture imports 60 blocks with ten transactions each; the current head
+and 50 historical reports match direct RPC. Both API routes preserve current
+transaction hashes, all forty chart counts remain ten, and four browser
+checkpoints cover normal/pinned/expired/recovered views.
+
+An offline measurement retains 2,000 heights, eight identities and nine variants
+at each height: 18,000 compact fork records. Heap growth after explicit GC is
+about 13–14 MiB for both one and 714 transactions per block. Full cache JSON is
+14,195,884 and 14,235,884 bytes respectively; the difference is the count's two
+extra decimal digits at each serialized record/reference. Advancing beyond the
+window releases old records. This is not peak RSS, concurrent collector load or
+a production budget. Larger input, current-head output/snapshot sizes, enrollment
+counts and proxy limits still require an aligned measured profile.
+
+The first actual integration attempt failed its freshness assertion without
+recording the receipt values. Readiness now waits for all three report kinds
+and saves initial payloads and timestamps. The subsequent run passes without
+widening freshness limits. The original failure's exact cause is unproven;
+do not treat this as a soak or timing-stability proof.
+
+No production efsn, consensus, database schema, dependency or frontend changes
+are included. The prior compiled frontend is reused with its build/source hashes
+checked. Review artifacts and raw attempts are preserved in the node repository's
+`docs/evidence/restart-dashboard-retention-2026-10-03/` bundle.
